<%--
Text_Viewer

Displays a plain-text attachment AS ITSELF - the file's own characters in a scrolling frame -
rather than a generic extension icon. Registered for `text/*` in
config('rsx.preview.viewers').

WHY THE EXTRACTION AND NOT THE RAW BYTES. The text pipeline has already read this file, decoded
whatever encoding it arrived in, and capped it at config('rsx.search.max_text_bytes'). Fetching
the inline URL instead would hand the browser undecoded bytes of unknown charset and an unbounded
length, and would need its own realtime wiring to notice the pipeline finishing. Reading the
extraction gets a decoded, bounded string and the swap for free. The cost is honest and stated in
the waiting state: a just-uploaded file shows "(Extracting Text...)" for the second or two before
the worker reaches it.

Extraction runs a moment AFTER the upload, so this viewer owns a WAITING state: it subscribes to
its attachment id in on_create() and swaps to the text when the worker's frame arrives - no
reload, no polling. Same shape as its sibling <Document_Text_Preview>, which renders the extracted
text of a file whose PICTURE is the preview; this one renders a file whose text IS the preview.

States, in the order the template tests them:
  error_data set        the endpoint refused, or the attachment is gone   -> (Text Unavailable)
  info === null         the first load has not answered yet               -> (Loading...)
  status 'pending'      no index row yet - extraction is queued           -> (Extracting Text...)
  status 'error'        extraction failed (terminal) or a degraded upload -> (Text Unavailable)
  status 'unsupported'  nothing extractable                              -> (Text Unavailable)
  status 'available'
    with empty text     the file is genuinely empty                       -> (This file is empty)
    with text           the text, newlines and space runs preserved (long lines soft-wrap)

Args (the viewer contract Document_Preview passes to every dynamic viewer):
  $attachment_id (required) - the File_Attachment_Model id to read. Throws if absent.
  $file_name     (optional) - shown in no state; accepted so the contract matches its siblings.

Events OUT: 'preview_loaded' {pages:1}. Single "page"; set_page is a no-op.

TYPOGRAPHY IS MONOSPACE, DELIBERATELY, and this is where it differs from Document_Text_Preview.
That component inherits the host's font by contract, because it presents PROSE extracted from a
document. This one presents a FILE - logs, CSV, config, code - where a monospace column
matters, so the frame fixes a monospace family rather than inheriting one. It still SOFT-WRAPS a
long line rather than clipping it, exactly as Document_Text_Preview does.
--%>
<Define:Text_Viewer tag="div">
    <% if (!this.args.attachment_id) { throw new Error('Text_Viewer requires $attachment_id'); } %>
    <%
        const _info = this.data.error_data ? { status: 'error', text: null } : this.data.info;
        const _available = _info && _info.status === 'available' && _info.text !== null;
        const _has_text = _available && _info.text !== '';
    %>
    <div class="Text_Viewer__frame">
        <% if (!_info) { %>
            <div class="Text_Viewer__notice">(Loading...)</div>
        <% } else if (_info.status === 'pending') { %>
            <%-- The realtime subscription in on_create brings us back here the moment the
                 worker finishes the extraction pass. --%>
            <div class="Text_Viewer__notice">(Extracting Text...)</div>
        <% } else if (_available && !_has_text) { %>
            <%-- Distinguished from the failure notice below ON PURPOSE: an empty file is a
                 fact about the file, not a fault in the preview, and telling the reader
                 "unavailable" would send them looking for a problem that does not exist. --%>
            <div class="Text_Viewer__notice">(This file is empty)</div>
        <% } else if (!_has_text) { %>
            <%-- error / unsupported. Both are the same answer to the reader: there is no text
                 to show. The operator distinguishes them with rsx:search:reindex --status. --%>
            <div class="Text_Viewer__notice">(Text Unavailable)</div>
        <% } else { %>
            <%-- Escaped interpolation, NOT htmlbr(): the frame is white-space:pre-wrap, so the
                 newlines already render as line breaks and htmlbr() would add a <br> BESIDE each
                 surviving one and double-space the whole file. pre-wrap also preserves the runs
                 of spaces that carry column alignment, which nl2br cannot express. --%>
            <div class="Text_Viewer__text"><%= this.data.info.text %></div>
        <% } %>
    </div>
</Define:Text_Viewer>
